  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nI wish there was good news to discuss in the forecast, but this\nis not the case. A stagnant weather pattern will fully settle in\nover the region, locking in similar weather for each day. A \nlarge upper-level high pressure has parked over the Gulf Coast. \nThe central Plains and Midwest resides on the northern periphery\nof this feature. As a result, hot temperatures and periodic \nshowers and thunderstorms are to be expected each day. The most \nlikely timing for any showers and thunderstorms will be \novernight with the aid of the low-level jet and warm advection. \nOtherwise, daytime heat keeps a cap in place and thunderstorms \nat bay.\n\nEarlier morning showers and thunderstorms have lifted out of \nthe area, leaving behind a sunny, but humid, afternoon. \nTemperatures should climb well into the 90s with heat index \nvalues approaching 100. Another round of showers and \nthunderstorms is anticipated tonight, especially over northeast \nNebraska. With humid conditions and clouds, temperatures \nstruggle to cool into the lower 70s. Any rain should dissipate \nor exit the region during the late morning hours on Tuesday. \nTuesday will likely see temperatures a touch higher than today, \nbut dewpoint temperatures may decrease slightly. In the end, \nheat index values near 100 are likely once again. Heading into \nTuesday night, the next round of showers and thunderstorms is \nexpected. This round may have a slightly better environment to \nwork with, so a low chance of damaging winds and large hail \naccompanies any stronger thunderstorms. Temperatures struggle to\ncool with continued rain and clouds with Wednesday morning \ntemperatures in the 70s and a bit warmer than the previous \nmorning. \n\nWednesday and beyond is much of the same as the previous days, \nbut the heat may increase further. With the prolonged nature of \nthe heat, heat products may need to be considered. While the \ncurrent forecast does trend temperatures down slightly to close \nout the week, long-range ensemble guidance is indicating that \nthe worst of the heat may linger into the weekend. In this case,\na prolonged heatwave would likely require heat products, \nespecially as overnight temperatures offer limited relief. \nOutside of the heat, at least a low chance of overnight showers \nand thunderstorms continues to be the norm.",
